    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "[auto-routed: surprise=1.00] Implement the rigid and fat constraint families as required by the capacity_scan interface, ensuring the rigid family is a low-dimensional linear system (e.g., linear constraints with unique solution) and the fat family is a high-dimensional continuous family (e.g., linear constraints with null space). This will enable testing the hypothesis that small networks fail to reach zero violation for rigid families due to optimization artifacts. The family function must return properly shaped training/validation data without overlap, using scipy.optimize.minimize with L-BFGS-B for optimization (as per protocol). The code should be saved to /workspace/research_outputs/shusheng_rsi_run26/family.py so the existing run() can import it."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "skill",
      "value": "None"
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "expected_prediction",
      "value": "The rigid family will show N_c(w) that is non-increasing (flat or decreasing) as w increases, indicating low-dimensional solution space. The fat family will show N_c(w) increasing with w, indicating high-dimensional solution space. For the rigid family, we expect to see zero violation achieved at small widths (e.g., N_c ≈ 2-4) across different w values. For the fat family, we expect N_c(w) to increase with w (e.g., from N_c=4 at w=2 to N_c=8 at w=5). The training error should be ≤1e-4 and validation error ≤1e-3 for successful zero-violation cases."
    },
